# Vitis build artifacts
*.xo
*.xclbin
*.log
*.jou
*.xml
*.json
# Calibration libraries are tracked — `publish_calib` writes the distilled corpus (mostly *.json)
# into them and its diff must be reviewable, so they stay visible despite the blanket *.json ignore
# above.  BOTH homes are re-included:
#   waveflow/calib/platforms/  the reference library shipped as package data
#   calib/platforms/           a PROJECT's own library — the default `platforms_root`, so this is
#                              where a user following the documented workflow publishes.  Without
#                              this line their library is silently ignored, which defeats the point
#                              of publishing at all.  (The untracked work tier is **/calib/work/,
#                              ignored separately below.)
!waveflow/calib/platforms/**
!**/calib/platforms/**
# The `**/` matters: `calib/platforms/**` contains a slash, so git anchors it to the repo
# root -- an example's library at examples/<name>/calib/platforms/ stayed ignored, with only
# a few files force-added by hand.  That was invisible until a key change renamed 30 module
# directories: the deletions staged, the replacements did not, and a fresh clone would have
# had a store with records but no identities.
*.o
*.obj
*.exe
*.elf
*.bin
*.map
*.vitis/
_build/
_build.hw/
_build.sw/
# NOTE: keep this anchored to the Vitis csim output. A bare `build/` matches ANY directory named
# `build` at any depth, which silently swallows source dirs (tests/build/, docs/guide/build/,
# waveflow/build/). See the Distribution artifacts section below.
**/csim/build/
*.db
*.pb
**/dfx_runtime.txt
*summary*
*.rpt
.autopilot/
*.aps
*.directive
syn/
impl/
.debug/
*.app
*.xdc




